What we're building

The retrieval index: a searchable pool of real, historical Delta customer-complaint → resolution pairs, so that at agent-runtime we can find "what similar issue happened before, and how was it handled" for any new incoming message. This is the mechanism that makes "grounded" true rather than assumed.

Design decisions baked into this, following from everything we've already established:

Pool source: only pairs where is_thread_root == true AND is_dm_redirect == false — genuine opening complaints paired with real (non-redirect) resolutions. We have 34,435 candidates; after this filter we'll have fewer, but still comfortably more than our target.
Pool size: ~2,500 (our earlier decision), randomly sampled with a fixed seed from the filtered pool — if the filtered pool ever comes in under 2,500, use all of it rather than erroring.
No vector database, no FAISS — at 2,500 vectors, brute-force cosine similarity via plain NumPy matrix multiplication is fast enough and avoids an unnecessary dependency. This is the "simplest alternative that's still reasonable" call from our architecture doc.
Caching from day one — every embedding call gets cached to disk by a hash of its text, written incrementally (not just at the end). This matters more here than it sounds: if the script crashes or hits a rate limit partway through embedding 2,500 texts, a rerun should resume from cache instead of re-paying for everything from scratch.
