Edge cases identified
Edge case	Handling
GOOGLE_API_KEY not set	Fail immediately with a clear, specific error message — not a cryptic SDK exception
Script crashes/rate-limited mid-run	Cache is written incrementally per-text, so a rerun resumes from where it left off instead of re-embedding everything
Filtered pool smaller than target pool size (2,500)	Use all available pairs, log a clear warning, don't crash
Transient API error (network blip, rate limit)	Retry with exponential backoff, small fixed number of attempts, then fail loudly rather than silently returning bad data
Same text embedded twice (e.g. rerun, or appears in both pool-building and a later query)	Cache hit — reuses the stored vector, no wasted API call
API returns embeddings in unexpected order/count vs. input	Explicit length check against the input batch; raise an error rather than silently mismatching text to vector

Reviewing the design notes
Isolating _call_embed_api from _embed_batch_with_retry — right call. This means the retry/backoff logic and the caching logic can be tested independently of each other, which is cleaner than mocking the whole SDK.
CACHE_DIR as a bare module-level Path, referenced by name (not imported elsewhere) — this is the correct pattern, and it's worth knowing why it's correct for your interview: if another file had done from gemini_client import CACHE_DIR, that would copy the value at import time, and monkeypatch.setattr(gemini_client, "CACHE_DIR", tmp_path) would silently fail to affect it — the test would look like it passed while actually writing to your real cache directory. Referencing it as gemini_client.CACHE_DIR everywhere is what makes the monkeypatch actually take effect. Good instinct.
The cache-hit/API-call breakdown living inside embed_texts()'s print rather than being duplicated in build_index.py's summary — this is Claude Code correctly noticing a small contradiction in my own spec (I asked for the breakdown in both places without giving embed_texts a way to return it), and resolving it sensibly rather than duplicating output or silently changing the return type. That's a good sign — it means it's reasoning about consistency, not just pattern-matching instructions.

test_embed_texts_preserves_input_order_across_cache_and_misses existing as its own explicit test is the one I'd have specifically worried about if it were missing — mixing cached and freshly-fetched results back into the original order is exactly the kind of thing that's easy to get subtly wrong (an off-by-one, or matching by "which finished first" instead of by position). Good that it's covered.

