The issue: embeddings need to know if they're a "document" or a "query"

Gemini's embedding model supports a task_type parameter — RETRIEVAL_DOCUMENT for things being indexed, RETRIEVAL_QUERY for the incoming search text. This isn't a minor tuning knob: asymmetric retrieval models like this one are specifically trained so that a document embedding and a query embedding for the same underlying meaning land close together in vector space, but a document embedded as if it were a query (or vice versa) can retrieve noticeably worse. Right now, gemini_client.py doesn't set task_type at all — everything defaults to whatever Gemini uses when it's unspecified, and query.py would embed an incoming customer message the exact same way build_index.py embeds the historical pool. That's the mismatch.

Why this has to be fixed now, specifically

The cache key is hashlib.sha256(f"{model}:{text}") — it doesn't include task_type. So here's the failure mode if we ran the real build today and fixed this later: we'd cache 2,500 embeddings under (model, text) keys with no task type. Later, if we add task_type to fix retrieval quality, those same (model, text) keys would still exist in the cache — so a rerun would silently reuse the old, wrong-task-type embeddings instead of regenerating them, and we might not notice for a while, since nothing would error. Catching this before the first real API call is free; catching it after means either blowing away the whole cache or shipping a real, hidden correctness bug.